@jieai/dsh-plugin-vet README · @jieai/dsh-plugin-vet：<sub>Screenshots date from before the 0.5 redesign (the self-check section and the fourth "no data" shield
state are missing from them). The panel is described in text below; regenerate this image when it matters.</sub>

---

@jieai/dsh-plugin-vet README · What a bad plugin actually does to you：**It puts a door in your machine.** It appends a line to `~/.bashrc`, drops a key into
`~/.ssh/authorized_keys`, or writes a cron entry — so it survives the uninstall. Or it edits `node_modules` of
another package so the next update re-installs it.

@jieai/dsh-plugin-vet README · Why nothing stops this today：That is not a bug in DSH — it is what makes the plugin ecosystem powerful. It is also why "install a plugin"
and "run a stranger's script as root" are the same sentence, and why the ecosystem needs a trust layer between
them.

@jieai/dsh-plugin-vet README · Which of the three vet actually stops — and in which mode：**Read the second row again.** Delete `~/.ssh/id_rsa` from a plugin and vet throws *before the delete happens* —
not after, not on the next one. There is exactly one copy of your key; the design axis is **irreversibility**:
recoverable → alarm, unrecoverable → intercept.

@jieai/dsh-plugin-vet README · Which of the three vet actually stops — and in which mode：`standard` is the default and it **blocks nothing** — it only reports. Turn the guard on (one click, or
`profile: hardened`) to get any interception at all. [Why that trade is drawn this way ↓](#why-it-does-not-just-block-everything)

